- How would you ensure type-safety across multiple dynamically loaded backends?
- What strategies would you use to unload or hot-swap a backend without dropping in-flight events?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design extensible CLI architectures with runtime plugin support and graceful user experience.

### Key Concepts
- **Command pattern**: Encapsulates operations as first-class objects
- **Registry**: Enables dynamic command registration and lookup
- **Alias support**: Handles naming conflicts and provides convenient shortcuts
- **Auto-generated help**: Constructed from command metadata
- **Graceful failure**: Provides helpful error messages for unrecognized commands

### Code Example
```typescript
interface CommandContext {
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  stdin: NodeJS.ReadableStream;
  stdout: NodeJS.WritableStream;
  stderr: NodeJS.WritableStream;
}

interface CommandDefinition {
  name: string;
  aliases: string[];
  description: string;
  parse(args: string[]): Record<string, unknown>;
  execute(ctx: CommandContext): Promise<void> | void;
}

class CommandRegistry {
  private commands = new Map<string, CommandDefinition>();

  register(cmd: CommandDefinition): void {
    this.commands.set(cmd.name, cmd);
    cmd.aliases.forEach(alias => this.commands.set(alias, cmd));
  }

  resolve(name: string): CommandDefinition | undefined {
    return this.commands.get(name);
  }

  getCommands(): CommandDefinition[] {
    return [...new Set(this.commands.values())];
  }

  generateHelp(): string {
    const commands = this.getCommands();
    const longestName = Math.max(...commands.map(c => c.name.length));
    return [
      'Usage: cli <command> [options]',
      '',
      'Commands:',
      ...commands.map(c => `  ${c.name.padEnd(longestName)}  ${c.description}`),
    ].join('\n');
  }
}

async function main(argv: string[]) {
  const [cmdName, ...args] = argv.slice(2);
  const registry = new CommandRegistry();

  // Register commands
  registry.register({
    name: 'greet',
    aliases: ['g'],
    description: 'Greet a user',
    parse(args) {
      const name = args[0] || 'World';
      return { name };
    },
    execute({ args, stdout }) {
      stdout.write(`Hello, ${args.name}!\n`);
    },
  });

  if (!cmdName) {
    console.log(registry.generateHelp());
    process.exit(0);
  }

  const cmd = registry.resolve(cmdName);
  if (!cmd) {
    console.error(`Error: Unknown command '${cmdName}'`);
    console.error(registry.generateHelp());
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const parsedArgs = cmd.parse(args);
  cmd.execute({
    args: parsedArgs,
    stdin: process.stdin,
    stdout: process.stdout,
    stderr: process.stderr,
  });
}

main(process.argv);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin system with concurrency considerations and clear trade-offs between design patterns.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy Pattern
- Registry/Service Locator
- Atomic/lock-free swaps for runtime configuration
- Deterministic ordering and starvation considerations
- Per-queue isolation in a multi-worker setting

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Strategy {
  dispatch(queueId: string, tasks: any[]): any[]
}

class Registry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, T>()
  register(name: string, value: T) { this.map.set(name, value) }
  get(name: string): T | undefined { return this.map.get(name) }
}

class QueueEngine {
  private currentStrategy: Strategy;
  constructor(private registry: Registry<Strategy>, initial: string) {
    const s = registry.get(initial); if (!s) throw new Error('unknown strategy')
    this.currentStrategy = s
  }
  swapStrategy(name: string) {
    const s = this.registry.get(name); if (s) this.currentStrategy = s
  }
  dispatch(queueId: string, tasks: any[]) {
    return this.currentStrategy.dispatch(queueId, tasks)
  }
}

// Usage
class RoundRobin implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }
class WorkStealing implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }
class PriorityBased implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }

const reg = new Registry<Strategy>()
reg.register('rr', new RoundRobin())
reg.register('ws', new WorkStealing())
reg.register('pb', new PriorityBased())

const engine = new QueueEngine(reg, 'rr')
engine.swapStrategy('pb')
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test swapping under peak load?
- How to ensure per-queue isolation when multiple queues swap strategies concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores runtime extensibility and safe composition of resilience patterns (Strategy + Decorator) at scale, mirroring needs in large tech orgs where policies evolve without touching core call logic.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern: policy abstraction
- Chain of Responsibility / Decorator: pipeline composition
- Registry pattern: runtime extensibility
- Trade-offs: backoffs, timeout precision, isolation

## Code Example

```typescript
type Request = { url: string; payload?: any }
type Response = any
type Next = () => Promise<Response>

interface Policy {
  handle(req: Request, next: Next): Promise<Response>
}
type PolicyFactory = (ctx?: any) => Policy

class PolicyRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, PolicyFactory>()
  register(name: string, factory: PolicyFactory) { this.map.set(name, factory) }
  create(name: string, ctx?: any): Policy | null { const f = this.map.get(name); return f ? f(ctx) : null }
}
```

